cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use memorywire, please cite it as below."
title: "memorywire: A vendor-neutral wire format for agent memory operations"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Munirathinam"
    given-names: "Thamilvendhan"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-5641-7424"
    email: "mthamil107@gmail.com"
version: 0.0.0
date-released: 2026-05-26
# NOTE: `version:` and `date-released:` above are managed by release-please
# (see .github/release-please-config.json — CITATION.cff is in `extra-files`).
# Do NOT bump them by hand; merging the release-PR will rewrite them.
#
# The memorywire paper is on arXiv (2606.01138). No Zenodo software DOI yet;
# wire the GitHub<->Zenodo integration to mint one if you want per-release DOIs.
identifiers:
  - type: other
    value: "arXiv:2606.01138"
    description: "arXiv preprint identifier (memorywire paper)"
url: "https://github.com/mthamil107/memorywire"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mthamil107/memorywire"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - agent
  - memory
  - wire-format
  - vendor-neutral
  - governance
  - RAG
  - vector-store
  - MCP
abstract: >-
  memorywire is a vendor-neutral wire format and reference implementation for agent
  memory operations. It defines five operations (remember, recall, forget, merge,
  expire) over four memory types (semantic, episodic, procedural, emotional), a
  `MemoryStore` protocol that any backend can implement, and an optional
  governance channel for human-in-the-loop diff-and-approve review of writes.
  memorywire composes above existing memory frameworks (mem0, Letta, Cognee, Zep,
  sqlite-vec, pgvector) rather than replacing them, with a memory router that
  fans operations across stores and fuses recall results via Reciprocal Rank
  Fusion (k=60) with an optional one-hop graph boost.
